1
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,310
I was eight the summer the Sun went out.

2
00:00:06,530 --> 00:00:08,320
I was drawing the Sun.

3
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,525
Who draws at six in the morning?

4
00:00:11,895 --> 00:00:13,055
MIRA: Artists.

5
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:16,280
And Biscuit was planning a crime.

6
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:19,835
At that moment, half the
world was in sunlight.

7
00:00:25,205 --> 00:00:26,595
Biscuit, no!

8
00:00:27,205 --> 00:00:28,940
Then the Sun went out.

9
00:00:33,210 --> 00:00:34,650
MIRA: Is it night again?

10
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:38,295
It's seven in the morning.

11
00:00:41,360 --> 00:00:44,070
The lights still worked. The Sun didn't.

12
00:00:44,440 --> 00:00:47,050
Then someone knocked. Three times.

13
00:00:47,420 --> 00:00:51,335
MR. ITO: Good morning. Shoes
on, please. Both of you.

14
00:00:51,705 --> 00:00:57,215
Mr. Ito, from upstairs. He was
seventy-three, and he was never in a hurry.

15
00:00:57,585 --> 00:01:01,520
His bag was already packed. It
had been packed for years.

16
00:01:01,890 --> 00:01:05,055
MR. ITO: And bring the dog.
He has your toast.

17
00:01:05,425 --> 00:01:07,915
It took me a year to understand why.

18
00:01:09,085 --> 00:01:11,845
Outside, the whole street was looking up.

19
00:01:12,215 --> 00:01:13,800
It's just an eclipse!

20
00:01:14,215 --> 00:01:18,380
MR. ITO: Eclipses are polite.
They're over in seven minutes.

21
00:01:18,750 --> 00:01:21,835
It was happening everywhere,
at the same moment.

22
00:01:22,505 --> 00:01:25,090
In New York, it was two in the morning.

23
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:29,045
Most of them woke up to a
morning that never came.

24
00:01:29,815 --> 00:01:33,455
MR. ITO: Up to the roof. Kai,
bring my telescope.

25
00:01:33,825 --> 00:01:38,010
Kai carried it up six floors. He
complained about every one.

26
00:01:38,380 --> 00:01:42,640
Mr. Ito came up here every
morning to watch the sunrise.

27
00:01:43,010 --> 00:01:45,545
That morning, he had seen the last one.

28
00:01:46,195 --> 00:01:47,960
MR. ITO: Venus first.

29
00:01:48,330 --> 00:01:50,145
MIRA: Why is that one still on?

30
00:01:50,515 --> 00:01:56,905
MR. ITO: Venus has no light of its own. It's the
Sun's light, coming the long way round.

31
00:01:57,275 --> 00:02:01,815
If it happened at the Sun, Venus
goes out at seven-oh-four.

32
00:02:02,185 --> 00:02:03,575
So we waited.

33
00:02:07,345 --> 00:02:09,935
MR. ITO: Then it happened at the Sun.

34
00:02:10,705 --> 00:02:14,245
Jupiter is further out. Eight-oh-four.

35
00:02:14,615 --> 00:02:17,080
At four minutes past eight, it did.

36
00:02:17,850 --> 00:02:23,065
He tapped the telescope three times. He
always did that. I never asked why.

37
00:02:32,135 --> 00:02:36,750
(RADIO) Phone networks are down across
the city. Please stay indoors.

38
00:02:37,120 --> 00:02:39,280
Everyone's going to the shops.

39
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:42,540
MR. ITO: Then so are we.

40
00:02:43,050 --> 00:02:46,515
Kai was right. The whole
city had the same idea.

41
00:02:46,885 --> 00:02:49,400
Candles. Rice. Batteries. Water.

42
00:02:49,770 --> 00:02:51,535
MIRA: They're on Biscuit's list.

43
00:02:51,905 --> 00:02:55,415
Two grown men fought over the
last electric heater.

44
00:02:55,785 --> 00:02:59,250
MR. ITO: Heaters need
electricity. They'll learn.

45
00:02:59,620 --> 00:03:01,960
Kai? It’s Mum. Can you hear me?

46
00:03:02,880 --> 00:03:04,595
Mum! Are you okay?

47
00:03:04,965 --> 00:03:08,305
I'm fine, love. But I can't come home yet.

48
00:03:08,675 --> 00:03:12,415
The whole city is coming here.
They need every nurse.

49
00:03:12,785 --> 00:03:14,400
MIRA: When are you coming home?

50
00:03:14,785 --> 00:03:19,150
Soon. I'll call every night at
seven. Stay with Mr. Ito.

51
00:03:19,520 --> 00:03:23,005
She called every night at
seven. We never missed one.

52
00:03:23,375 --> 00:03:25,140
Is the Sun… dead?

53
00:03:25,810 --> 00:03:29,775
MR. ITO: If the Sun were gone,
its pull would be gone too.

54
00:03:30,145 --> 00:03:33,935
And tomorrow's tide would
be a third smaller.

55
00:03:35,055 --> 00:03:38,470
Go and look. High tide is at eleven.

56
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:42,505
So the next morning, Kai and I
walked down to the harbour.

57
00:03:42,875 --> 00:03:44,540
MIRA: It's right on the line!

58
00:03:44,910 --> 00:03:46,870
So the Sun's still there.

59
00:03:47,240 --> 00:03:48,650
MIRA: Then where is it?

60
00:03:49,320 --> 00:03:53,180
The Sun hadn't gone anywhere.
Something was in front of it.

61
00:03:54,050 --> 00:03:57,215
MIRA: The sea's growing a
skin. The fish don't mind!

62
00:03:57,585 --> 00:04:01,175
NEWS ANCHOR: Infrared telescopes have
found something around the Sun.

63
00:04:01,545 --> 00:04:05,560
It's warm. It's round. And it's
twice as wide as the Sun.

64
00:04:06,230 --> 00:04:08,365
There was something around the Sun.

65
00:04:08,735 --> 00:04:11,050
A shell. Wrapped around a star.

66
00:04:12,220 --> 00:04:15,360
NEWS ANCHOR: Governments are
asking everyone to stay calm.

67
00:04:15,710 --> 00:04:17,350
Nobody was calm.

68
00:04:18,120 --> 00:04:20,230
MIRA: Mr. Ito, who built it?

69
00:04:21,750 --> 00:04:24,165
MR. ITO: It's bedtime, Mira.

70
00:04:24,535 --> 00:04:26,620
It was four in the afternoon.

71
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:29,760
He'd never sent me to bed before.

72
00:04:30,530 --> 00:04:35,470
(RADIO) …a solar storm that left the Sun
four days ago, arriving tonight.

73
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:37,505
MIRA: It's saying goodbye.

74
00:04:44,675 --> 00:04:47,385
On Day 7, the power went out.

75
00:04:47,755 --> 00:04:51,540
Not just ours. All over the
world, the grids gave out.

76
00:04:51,910 --> 00:04:55,320
Mr. Ito showed us how to
make one warm room.

77
00:04:55,690 --> 00:04:58,275
Free fridge. Finally, some good news.

78
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:02,075
That night, Kai pretended
he wasn't scared.

79
00:05:02,445 --> 00:05:04,860
He was holding my hand too hard.

80
00:05:05,730 --> 00:05:07,470
Then we smelled smoke.

81
00:05:07,840 --> 00:05:11,055
MR. ITO: They're burning
furniture. Indoors.

82
00:05:11,425 --> 00:05:14,965
Everyone out! It's the gas you can't see.

83
00:05:15,335 --> 00:05:17,920
Mr. Ito got the whole floor out.

84
00:05:18,290 --> 00:05:20,850
At the street door, Biscuit panicked.

85
00:05:21,220 --> 00:05:22,355
MIRA: BISCUIT!

86
00:05:23,725 --> 00:05:27,490
Mira, listen to me.
Biscuit is a clever dog.

87
00:05:27,860 --> 00:05:30,625
Clever dogs find warm places.

88
00:05:31,395 --> 00:05:33,485
MIRA: Kai. What’s warm out there?

89
00:05:33,835 --> 00:05:35,620
…The metro. The vents.

90
00:05:36,290 --> 00:05:37,955
Biscuit! Come on, boy!

91
00:05:38,325 --> 00:05:42,465
Kai searched all night. Then
again, it was always night now.

92
00:05:45,035 --> 00:05:47,120
Paw prints. Fresh ones.

93
00:05:47,490 --> 00:05:50,475
They led to the only warm
thing on the street.

94
00:05:52,595 --> 00:05:54,760
You stupid, brilliant dog.

95
00:05:55,130 --> 00:05:59,190
A few metres down, the ground
stays around ten degrees.

96
00:05:59,560 --> 00:06:03,120
Biscuit hadn't run away. He'd
found the way down.

97
00:06:03,490 --> 00:06:07,075
By morning, half the street had
followed him underground.

98
00:06:07,445 --> 00:06:10,755
All over the world, cities
moved underground.

99
00:06:11,125 --> 00:06:14,910
By Day 30, Harbour Street
station was a town.

100
00:06:15,280 --> 00:06:20,015
We grew food under pink lamps.
Everyone got very sick of lettuce.

101
00:06:20,385 --> 00:06:24,770
At seven every morning, the
engineers turned on the big lamps.

102
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,280
MR. ITO: We'll call it sunrise.

103
00:06:28,400 --> 00:06:32,060
MIRA: It's yellow. And round. And
it's warm on your face.

104
00:06:32,430 --> 00:06:35,715
Some of them had already
forgotten what colour it was.

105
00:06:36,685 --> 00:06:39,350
I kept mine in my pocket, all year.

106
00:06:40,120 --> 00:06:43,285
Biscuit got a fan club.
And a bigger belly.

107
00:06:49,855 --> 00:06:52,090
MIRA: Mr. Ito. Who built it?

108
00:06:52,460 --> 00:06:55,220
Nobody builds a thing that size in a day.

109
00:06:55,590 --> 00:06:57,705
MR. ITO: No. Nobody did.

110
00:06:59,190 --> 00:07:03,830
Nineteen-nineteen. A total
eclipse. Look at the lines.

111
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:08,765
It was always there. Edge-on,
like the slats of a blind.

112
00:07:09,135 --> 00:07:13,275
On the morning the Sun went
out, every slat turned.

113
00:07:14,045 --> 00:07:16,160
MIRA: You've seen those lines before.

114
00:07:16,530 --> 00:07:20,270
MR. ITO: Once. A long time
ago. Nobody listened.

115
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:25,000
I believed him. It was true. It
just wasn’t all of it.

116
00:07:25,970 --> 00:07:29,335
On Day 100, the generators failed.

117
00:07:29,705 --> 00:07:33,070
MR. ITO: I know a little
about old machines.

118
00:07:33,440 --> 00:07:36,330
Mira? Our generators are going too.

119
00:07:36,700 --> 00:07:39,315
If you can't hear me for a while, don't—

120
00:07:39,885 --> 00:07:41,400
MIRA: Mum? Mum!

121
00:07:43,485 --> 00:07:45,445
That was the coldest night.

122
00:07:45,815 --> 00:07:47,880
How does this end, Mira?

123
00:07:49,050 --> 00:07:51,065
MIRA: With the lights coming back on.

124
00:07:52,035 --> 00:07:57,470
On Day 180, Mr. Ito and the
engineers got them running again.

125
00:07:57,840 --> 00:08:01,805
Kai, Mira, it’s Mum. Our
power’s gone. Yours is back.

126
00:08:02,175 --> 00:08:05,690
So we're bringing everyone to
you. Across the river.

127
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:08,770
Mum, it's sixty below out there.

128
00:08:09,140 --> 00:08:11,430
Then we'd better walk fast.

129
00:08:12,200 --> 00:08:17,510
She walked two kilometres across the ice at
sixty below, with forty patients.

130
00:08:17,880 --> 00:08:19,645
And then she was home.

131
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:23,170
You kept them alive.

132
00:08:23,540 --> 00:08:25,530
MR. ITO: They kept me alive.

133
00:08:26,300 --> 00:08:27,760
MIRA: What are you building?

134
00:08:28,300 --> 00:08:30,515
MR. ITO: Something to listen with.

135
00:08:31,285 --> 00:08:34,700
NEWS ANCHOR: Astronomers have found a
beam of light leaving the shell.

136
00:08:35,070 --> 00:08:42,335
It's aimed at a star called HD 219134.
Twenty-one light-years away.

137
00:08:43,105 --> 00:08:44,620
MR. ITO: Twenty-one.

138
00:08:45,990 --> 00:08:47,900
I didn't ask what it meant.

139
00:08:48,570 --> 00:08:51,385
All the Sun's light, going to one star.

140
00:08:56,755 --> 00:09:02,765
On Day 365, we all went up to the
surface. One year, to the minute.

141
00:09:03,135 --> 00:09:06,649
MIRA: One whole lap around the
Sun. Happy birthday, Earth.

142
00:09:07,019 --> 00:09:10,934
MR. ITO: The beam's stopped.
Something's coming from the Sun.

143
00:09:11,304 --> 00:09:12,089
One.

144
00:09:12,839 --> 00:09:13,374
Two.

145
00:09:14,124 --> 00:09:14,859
Three.

146
00:09:15,929 --> 00:09:17,619
Then it didn't stop.

147
00:09:18,489 --> 00:09:20,849
That's not three. That's hundreds.

148
00:09:21,219 --> 00:09:25,009
MR. ITO: One thousand, six
hundred and seventy-nine.

149
00:09:25,679 --> 00:09:27,214
Is that a lot?

150
00:09:27,784 --> 00:09:31,974
MR. ITO: It's twenty-three times
seventy-three. Nothing else.

151
00:09:32,644 --> 00:09:34,634
MIRA: Like squares on graph paper.

152
00:09:35,284 --> 00:09:36,624
It's a picture!

153
00:09:37,494 --> 00:09:41,034
MR. ITO: Twenty-three across.
Seventy-three down.

154
00:09:41,494 --> 00:09:45,034
At the top, they'd finished
his count. Up to ten.

155
00:09:45,404 --> 00:09:48,639
Then them. And us, beside
them, very small.

156
00:09:49,409 --> 00:09:53,419
Their star. Their home. And our
Sun, inside its shell.

157
00:09:54,389 --> 00:09:56,524
MIRA: Mr. Ito? What is it?

158
00:09:56,894 --> 00:09:58,609
MR. ITO: It's a reply.

159
00:09:59,379 --> 00:10:00,394
MIRA: To who?

160
00:10:01,379 --> 00:10:02,669
MR. ITO: To me.

161
00:10:17,439 --> 00:10:22,354
The man in the observatory was Mr.
Ito. Forty-three years ago.

162
00:10:23,124 --> 00:10:26,339
He found the lines, and the
star they pointed to.

163
00:10:26,709 --> 00:10:30,949
They told him not to. He knocked
anyway. One. Two. Three.

164
00:10:31,719 --> 00:10:35,809
Twenty-one years to get there.
Twenty-one years to come back.

165
00:10:36,179 --> 00:10:39,819
MR. ITO: It's a telephone,
Mira. Theirs. I dialled it.

166
00:10:40,189 --> 00:10:42,679
And now they've answered.

167
00:10:43,449 --> 00:10:48,789
To call that far, it needed the Sun.
All of it. For one whole year.

168
00:10:49,159 --> 00:10:52,824
It sent them everything it
ever heard from us.

169
00:10:53,174 --> 00:10:54,309
MIRA: Like what?

170
00:10:54,879 --> 00:10:59,219
MR. ITO: Every song. Every
voice. A hundred years of us.

171
00:10:59,989 --> 00:11:01,824
MIRA: Did they know it would hurt us?

172
00:11:02,594 --> 00:11:04,109
MR. ITO: I don't know.

173
00:11:05,279 --> 00:11:07,444
I'm sorry, Mira.

174
00:11:08,814 --> 00:11:12,149
At the bottom of their picture
was an empty square.

175
00:11:12,919 --> 00:11:14,259
MIRA: It's our turn.

176
00:11:15,229 --> 00:11:20,044
MR. ITO: It's stopped sending. But it's
still shut. It's waiting for us.

177
00:11:20,414 --> 00:11:23,779
NEWS ANCHOR: World leaders still
cannot agree on what to say.

178
00:11:24,149 --> 00:11:26,909
Two days later, they were still arguing.

179
00:11:27,579 --> 00:11:30,994
MR. ITO: Every day we wait,
the Sun stays shut.

180
00:11:31,364 --> 00:11:33,199
So what do we even send?

181
00:11:34,919 --> 00:11:36,429
MIRA: Can we send this?

182
00:11:36,799 --> 00:11:39,014
MR. ITO: Yes. I think we can.

183
00:11:39,784 --> 00:11:42,244
My drawing from the morning it went out.

184
00:11:42,594 --> 00:11:46,154
A big yellow sun. And me,
standing under it.

185
00:11:47,024 --> 00:11:49,309
Are we even allowed to do this?

186
00:11:49,659 --> 00:11:51,374
MR. ITO: Probably not.

187
00:11:52,344 --> 00:11:57,029
Kai carried the transmitter up to the
surface. He didn’t complain once.

188
00:11:57,399 --> 00:12:03,014
MR. ITO: If we send it at 6:35,
the light gets back at 6:52.

189
00:12:03,384 --> 00:12:05,144
MIRA: The same minute it went out.

190
00:12:05,814 --> 00:12:09,749
He pressed the key, the
way he had in 1983.

191
00:12:10,119 --> 00:12:13,409
MR. ITO: Eight minutes to get
there. Eight to come back.

192
00:12:13,779 --> 00:12:16,264
Sixteen minutes and forty seconds.

193
00:12:17,034 --> 00:12:20,649
The whole world was looking at
the same patch of sky.

194
00:12:21,019 --> 00:12:22,534
MIRA: What if it doesn’t work?

195
00:12:22,884 --> 00:12:24,849
Then we'll wait together.

196
00:12:34,719 --> 00:12:35,979
MIRA: There it is!

197
00:12:37,149 --> 00:12:40,459
Everywhere it was day, the
Sun came back at once.

198
00:12:47,029 --> 00:12:51,364
At 6:52, the same minute it went
out, the Sun came back.

199
00:12:52,734 --> 00:12:57,619
Every morning for forty-three years,
Mr. Ito watched the sunrise.

200
00:12:58,589 --> 00:13:01,624
I used to think it was
because he liked it.

201
00:13:02,794 --> 00:13:04,884
He was waiting for an answer.

202
00:13:06,454 --> 00:13:09,364
In twenty-one years,
they'll get my drawing.

203
00:13:10,114 --> 00:13:11,879
I wonder what they'll say.

204
00:13:13,249 --> 00:13:16,109
What would you have sent?
Tell us in the comments.
